IBM Bob was used as the AI-assisted development copilot for building GaonRide AI from concept to a complete working application. The development process followed Bob’s Ask, Plan, and Agent modes. In Ask mode, Bob was used to understand the project requirements, identify the major components, and decide how features such as voice-based trip requests, AI extraction, ride matching, driver coordination, and location services should work together. In Plan mode, Bob helped create the complete application architecture, including the FastAPI backend, SQLite database, frontend pages, API structure, AI services, and the workflow for matching passengers travelling on similar routes. In Agent mode, Bob assisted in generating and organizing the frontend and backend code, creating database models and APIs, integrating Groq-based speech transcription and AI extraction, configuring environment variables, implementing the deterministic ride-matching logic, and connecting map and routing services such as OpenStreetMap. Bob was also used to identify errors, refine the implementation, test different modules, and improve the overall project structure. This significantly reduced repetitive development work and allowed the team to focus more on the real-world problem being solved: helping people in rural areas coordinate shared transportation efficiently. IBM Bob therefore supported the complete software development lifecycle of GaonRide AI rather than simply being added as a standalone feature.
